+/*
+ * 0248: one argument, four experiments, and the control for the instrument.
+ *
+ * 0247's fault probe said "the store did not run", but that verdict assumes the
+ * SMMU bank we read reports a GPU data fault. It has never been shown to: the
+ * 0234 fault was an instruction fetch (or the CP's preload of it), the 0224
+ * FARs were garbage already sitting in the bank before any packet was sent, and
+ * sGFSR has never left zero. A channel that never reports a fault makes every
+ * "no fault" verdict worthless, so the control has to be a write we know the
+ * GPU performs - CP_EVENT_WRITE to the same unmapped page, the exact packet the
+ * fence uses and the exact page the descriptors already aim at.
+ *
+ *   polariscsp=<n>   bits 0:1 choose the stg immediate, bit 2 arms the control
+ *     0  result page          1  VA 0
+ *     2  page with no PTE     3  result page + 4 (word 1)
+ *     4  the CP control write, on top of whichever target bits 0:1 selected
+ *
+ * VA 0 is the interesting one. If stg is an a5xx-style descriptor-relative
+ * store, an immediate of zero writes word 0 of the result page and the sentinel
+ * finally appears; if it is the a6xx absolute-VA store that ir3_a6xx.c emits,
+ * zero is a page with no PTE and the SMMU has to answer. Either outcome says
+ * more than another register readback.
+ *
+ * The same build also prints the ten grid registers after the dispatch. They
+ * have never been read back: they go out in a second context bunch behind
+ * CP_SET_MARKER, and a wave whose global or local size never landed is a wave
+ * with zero active threads - launch overhead, no stores, no faults, and a
+ * perfectly good fence. That is the one shape that fits every log so far.
+ */
+static uint32_t pg_cs_target_mode;
+static bool pg_cs_cpctl;
 
